<code>assert (0 <= index && index <= \old(this.size())) => (element == null || this.get(index).equals(element));</code>
--------------------
<code>assert index >= 0 && index <= \old(this.size()) => (element == null && this.get(index) == null) || this.get(index).equals(element);</code>
--------------------
<code>assert (0 <= index && index <= \old(this.size())) => (element == null) => (this.get(index) == null || this.get(index).equals(element));</code>

However, the above assertion doesn't fully capture the intended logic correctly. The correct interpretation should be that if the element being added is null, then the element at the specified index after the addition should be null. If the element being added is not null, then the element at the specified index after the addition should be equal to the element being added.

Let's refine it:

<code>assert (0 <= index && index <= \old(this.size())) => ((element == null) => (this.get(index) == null) && (element != null) => this.get(index).equals(element));</code>

But since the element at the specified index after the addition will always be the element being added (whether it's null or not), the assertion can be simplified to:

<code>assert (0 <= index && index <= \old(this.size())) => this.get(index).equals(element);</code>
